  <!-- 3. 24-48H DELTA -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The June 17 Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding collapsed on July 7-8 following a three-stage breakdown: Iran's IRGC struck three commercial tankers on July 6-7 (a Qatari LNG carrier, a Saudi crude carrier, and a third vessel off Oman); the US responded with retaliatory strikes on Sirik, Qeshm, and Bandar Abbas; and Trump declared the ceasefire "over" during the NATO summit in Ankara on July 8 (Al Jazeera, CBS News). Iran's IRGC subsequently claimed strikes on 85 US military installations in Bahrain and Kuwait; CENTCOM confirmed US operations, though exact strike outcomes remain disputed across sources. Pakistan Prime Minister Sharif attended Khamenei's burial today in Mashhad and publicly stated Pakistan remains "ready to immediately re-facilitate dialogue" — the first formal diplomatic signal from a third-party mediator since the MOU collapse. → moved <span class="cite">F8</span>, <span class="cite">F10</span></p>

      <p>Hormuz transit traffic, as tracked by IMF PortWatch, ran at 34-45 vessels per day through early July — down from the 47-vessel pre-conflict average but not yet approaching the critical supply disruption threshold; the July 6-7 IRGC attacks caused a measurable AIS gap as vessels rerouted or switched off their AIS transponders, pushing the 7-day moving average toward the lower range. The UK/France MCM coalition, with logistics staged at Oman's Port of Duqm and RFA Lyme Bay designated as the mother ship, had finalized its operational mandate by July 6 (The National) — but the resumption of US-Iran hostilities on July 7-8 created an unsafe operating environment that has delayed active mine-clearing launch, with EU Aviation Safety Agency no-fly warnings now extended through August 31. → moved <span class="cite">F11</span></p>

      <p>The June 2026 diplomatic window produced three outputs, all now superseded by the July 7-8 MOU collapse: Pakistan's mediation sessions confirmed before June 16 (Al Jazeera June 15; Araghchi MFA statement June 16); the Burgenstock/Lucerne session of June 21, where Pakistan and Qatar served as named mediators and principals met face-to-face (NPR, Dawn.com June 21); and the CENTCOM-confirmed commencement of mine countermeasure operations on June 16, which remains technically active even as the MOU framework was declared defunct. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>Israel's coalition formally fell below the 61-seat Knesset majority threshold after the United Torah Judaism party withdrew over the Haredi military conscription dispute; the coalition now holds 60 seats in a 120-seat chamber. Knesset Legal Adviser Afik formally recommended July 17 as the dissolution date; both Knesset Speaker Ohana and coalition whip Katz confirmed this timetable (Times of Israel). The Central Elections Committee confirmed the election date as October 27 — the statutory latest, not an accelerated date — satisfying neither the sub-Oct-27 election date nor the final-reading-by-July-15 dissolution criteria for F6's YES outcome; F7's own OR-conditions (coalition below 61 seats, dissolution formally scheduled) are both confirmed met as of today. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span>, <span class="cite">F7</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 4. SCENARIO MAP -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Scenario Map
      <span class="meta">Computed from Forecast Board · 1 sentence per scenario</span>
