      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (45%):</strong> Anchored by F9 (IRGC Hormuz attack, p=0.70): the IRGC demonstrated willingness to strike commercial shipping during Khamenei's funeral procession, and the MOU collapse removes the political constraint on continued interdiction; IRGC expansion to Saudi/UAE territory (F12, p=0.25) constitutes the secondary escalation branch.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (35%):</strong> Neither side achieves decisive advantage — US strikes have degraded Iranian military capacity while Iran's Hormuz leverage constrains US economic tolerance; conflict settles into a low-intensity exchange pattern without a new ceasefire (F10 resolves NO) or full escalation (F12 resolves NO).</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (20%):</strong> Gated by F10 (new ceasefire by July 22, p=0.40): Pakistan's post-funeral diplomatic shuttle and economic pressure from oil prices at a 5% premium above pre-MOU levels create conditions for a bilateral framework, with the MCM mission (F11) as a potential subsequent de-escalation signal if a ceasefire holds.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5. INSIDE IRAN -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>IRGC state media (IRNA, Tasnim) covered Khamenei's six-day funeral procession — begun July 4 in Tehran and concluded in Mashhad today (July 9) — as an event of regime consolidation: live broadcast hours totaled more than any comparable event since Soleimani's 2020 funeral, with 30+ country delegations represented including Pakistan PM Sharif and ambassadors from China, India, and Russia. The state media framing emphasized continuity and institutional stability: Mojtaba Khamenei, installed as Supreme Leader in March, was absent from official procession footage, and IRNA carried no direct statements from him — consistent with the deliberately low public profile that has characterized his first months in office. → <span class="context-only">context only</span></p>

      <p>The IRGC's July 6-7 tanker strikes occurred within the funeral procession departure window — a timing that Fars and Tasnim framed as the IRGC acting as guardian of the revolution independent of the diplomatic track, not as a breach of the MOU. Both outlets described the strikes as enforcement of the Hormuz dual-corridor policy established under the elder Khamenei. Pezeshkian's Foreign Ministry made no public statement on the IRGC strikes during the funeral period, consistent with the MFA's stated freeze of diplomatic communications for the mourning period. Kayhan editorials characterized the IRGC's action as proof that the new leadership has inherited the resistance posture intact; the dominant question in state media after the burial is whether Mojtaba Khamenei will authorize resumed diplomacy or extend the IRGC's Hormuz confrontational posture. → moved <span class="cite">F9</span>, <span class="cite">F10</span></p>

      <p>On the nuclear question, IRNA and Press TV ran multiple items on FM Araghchi's formulation that enrichment represents "national pride" and that Iran has "no intention of surrendering to IAEA pressure." Kayhan called for formal NPT withdrawal in an editorial July 7, citing the destruction of Natanz and Fordow by US and Israeli strikes as evidence that "the NPT framework provided no protection." Tasnim separately noted that Pezeshkian's office reaffirmed a commitment to the nuclear provisions of the Islamabad MOU "in principle," leaving the nuclear posture formally ambiguous pending the new leadership's first substantive statement on the file — anticipated after the mourning period ends. → moved <span class="cite">F13</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

